  "content": {
    "id": "p35-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "$AB$ and $CD$ are two chords of a circle that intersect at $P$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p35-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\triangle APD$ is similar to $\\triangle CPB$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [
          {
            "id": "p35-q6-diagram",
            "role": "question",
            "format": "tikz",
            "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"geometry\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"source page 35 p35-q6-diagram\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[line width=0.8pt,draw=black,text=black,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\coordinate (O) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (A) at (140:2);\n\\coordinate (C) at (35:2);\n\\coordinate (B) at (-45:2);\n\\coordinate (D) at (210:2);\n\\coordinate (P) at (intersection of A--B and C--D);\n\\draw (O) circle (2);\n\\draw (A)--(B) (C)--(D) (A)--(D) (C)--(B);\n\\fill (P) circle (1.5pt);\n\\node[above left] at (A) {$A$};\n\\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};\n\\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};\n\\node[below left] at (D) {$D$};\n\\node[below] at (P) {$P$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
            "src": null,
            "widthMm": 48,
            "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
            "overlayOf": null,
            "transparent": false,
            "axes": null,
            "spec": {
              "sourcePage": 35,
              "sourceRegion": "Question 6a circle diagram",
              "description": "Unshaded circle with A upper left, C upper right, B lower right and D lower left; chords AB and CD intersect at marked point P. AD and CB complete the triangles.",
              "mathematics": "A, B, C and D lie on one circle. P is the intersection of AB and CD. Coordinates specify the drawing only and introduce no numerical givens.",
              "answerVisibility": "All geometry and labels are permanent student givens; no proof answers appear in the diagram."
            },
            "derived": false,
            "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
          }
        ],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\angle DAB=\\angle DCB$ and $\\angle ADC=\\angle ABC$ (angles subtended by the same chords). Also $\\angle APD=\\angle CPB$ (vertically opposite). Hence $\\triangle APD\\sim\\triangle CPB$ (equiangular).",
          "worked": "$\\angle DAB=\\angle DCB$ (angles subtended at the circumference by chord $DB$).\n\n$\\angle ADC=\\angle ABC$ (angles subtended at the circumference by chord $AC$).\n\nSince $A,P,B$ and $C,P,D$ are collinear, these give the corresponding angles at $A,C$ and $D,B$ in the two triangles.\n\n$\\angle APD=\\angle CPB$ (vertically opposite angles).\n\nTherefore $\\triangle APD\\sim\\triangle CPB$ (equiangular).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Write down all the equal ratios of sides in these two triangles.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\frac{AP}{PC}=\\frac{DP}{PB}=\\frac{AD}{CB}$",
          "worked": "The correspondence is $A\\leftrightarrow C$, $P\\leftrightarrow P$, $D\\leftrightarrow B$.\n\n$\\displaystyle\\frac{AP}{PC}=\\frac{DP}{PB}=\\frac{AD}{CB}$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence show that $AP\\times PB=DP\\times PC$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Cross-multiplying $\\dfrac{AP}{PC}=\\dfrac{DP}{PB}$ gives $AP\\times PB=DP\\times PC$.",
          "worked": "Using the first two ratios from part b,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{AP}{PC}&=\\frac{DP}{PB}\\\\AP\\times PB&=DP\\times PC.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
  },
